<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spandau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Spandau is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>. A town first recorded in 1197, with a castle of the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, it was where Elector Joachim II took communion in both kinds in 1539, marking Brandenburg's adoption of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Its 16th-century citadel survives, and its prison held Nazi war criminals sentenced at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spandau.jpg" alt="Spandau" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Savin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2013-08_View_from_Rathaus_Spandau_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Spandau is the westernmost of the twelve boroughs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, and extends along the western bank of the Havel. It has the smallest population of the boroughs but the fourth largest land area, 91.91 square kilometres. Outside Berlin it borders the districts of Oberhavel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelland">Havelland</a> and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The history of Spandau begins in the 7th or 8th century, when the Slavic Heveller settled the area and later built a fortress. King Henry I conquered it in 928, but it returned to Slavic rule after the rebellion of 983. In 1156 the Ascanian Albrecht the Bear took possession of the region. Spandau was first mentioned as Spandowe in 1197 in a deed of Otto II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>, forty years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colln">Cölln</a> part of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and it received city rights in 1232. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ii-hector">Joachim II of Brandenburg</a> completed the citadel, begun under Ascanian rule, between 1559 and 1594.</p><p>In 1634, during the Thirty Years' War, Spandau was surrendered to the Swedes, and in 1689 a commune of French Huguenots and Waldensians was established. French troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> took the city after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and Auerstedt in 1806, and Prussian and Russian troops besieged the citadel the following year. The revolutionary Gottfried Kinkel was held in the town prison from 1849 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-schurz">Carl Schurz</a> freed him on the night of 6 November 1850. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the arsenal made Spandau a centre of the German arms industry, and in 1920 it was incorporated into Greater Berlin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After World War II Spandau lay in the British sector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a>. Spandau Prison, rebuilt in 1876, held the Nazi war criminals given custodial sentences at the Nuremberg Trials. After the death of its last inmate, Rudolf Hess, in 1987, the Allied powers demolished it completely, and a shopping mall later replaced it. In the south of the district, Gatow airfield served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> during the occupation and the Berlin Airlift, and since 1995 has housed the Gatow Museum of Military History.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> citadel, the town hall of 1913 and the Catholic church of St Marien am Behnitz, built in 1848, are among its landmarks. A subcamp of the Sachsenhausen concentration camp, mainly for Polish and Hungarian women, operated there during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spandau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Joachim II introduced the Lutheran Reformation in 1539, taking communion in both kinds at <b>Spandau</b>, and the elector John Sigismund became a Calvinist in 1613, leaving a Reformed dynasty ruling a Lutheran people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
